-- Proposed ordinary-for protocol boundary; NOT an accepted language contract.
-- Sources: docs/control-flow/basic.md and
-- docs/algebraic-effect-and-delimited-continuation/algebraic-effect.md.
-- The current library Iterator.next protocol is not the lowering target.
-- This file is deliberately not installed in library/language-items.conf.

inline effect iteration_yield(item: T) where T;

trait Iter {
    assoc Item: .Type'builtin;
    inline fn iterate(self) -> #iteration_yield<Item> void;
}

trait IntoIterator {
    assoc Iterator: .Type'builtin;
    fn into_iter(self) -> Iterator;
}

-- Proposed selection rule, requiring confirmation:
-- 1. Evaluate the iterable exactly once, outside the loop binding scope.
-- 2. Prefer directly established Iter evidence. Otherwise select one
--    IntoIterator implementation and require Iter for its result type.
-- 3. Do not recursively search conversion chains or guess methods by spelling.
--    Driver-bound declaration identities select these protocols and the effect.
-- 4. Protocol validation requires the exact Item/yield payload relationship.
--    Conversion, iteration, and yielded values retain their resource contracts.

-- Lowering sketch (internal operations, not new surface syntax):
--   acquire iterable / converted iterator;
--   establish the original for-loop identity;
--   install the matching iteration_yield<Item> handler;
--   call the selected Iter.iterate method;
--   for each delivered item: match pattern, test guard, execute body;
--   release remaining iterator state on normal and nonlocal exits.
-- Proposed mismatch/false-guard behavior: discard this delivered item under
-- its normal cleanup obligations and resume the producer for the next item.

-- continue resumes this loop's producer after releasing the iteration scope.
-- break abandons that producer and exits this loop after required cleanup.
-- return and transfers to an outer loop retain their original lexical targets.
-- A yielded pointer does not acquire ownership of its pointee. Its lifetime
-- remains governed by the selected iterator's memory/effect contract.

-- Inline is an optimization intent in the current formal effect documentation.
-- Joint caller/callee/handler expansion should use the existing checked pass;
-- failure to inline retains the ordinary evidence path with the same behavior.
-- Do not promise mandatory expansion merely by recognizing a for statement.

-- Decisions still needed: public names/signatures; direct-Iter priority;
-- mismatch behavior; and whether IntoIterator carries the result-Iter bound
-- in its source declaration or as an explicitly checked language-item contract.
-- Until confirmed, ordinary for continues to retain its complete HIR payload
-- and report the existing unsupported-elaboration diagnostic at the NIR boundary.
